window.LESSONS = window.LESSONS || {};
window.LESSONS["netplus"] = {"1|OSI model":"# OSI model\n\nThe OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) model splits network communication into **seven layers**. Each layer has its own job and talks only to the layers directly above and below it. The exam uses it constantly: to describe devices, protocols, attacks, and to structure troubleshooting.\n\n## The seven layers\n\n| # | Layer | Job | PDU | Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 7 | Application | Network services for applications | Data | HTTP, DNS, SMTP, FTP, SSH |\n| 6 | Presentation | Formatting, encryption, compression | Data | TLS/SSL (per CompTIA), JPEG, ASCII |\n| 5 | Session | Sets up, manages, ends sessions | Data | RPC, NetBIOS session |\n| 4 | Transport | End-to-end delivery, ports, reliability | Segment (TCP) / Datagram (UDP) | TCP, UDP |\n| 3 | Network | Logical addressing and routing | Packet | IP, ICMP, IPsec, routers |\n| 2 | Data Link | Framing, MAC addressing, local delivery | Frame | Ethernet, 802.1Q, switches, ARP |\n| 1 | Physical | Bits on the medium: signals, cables, connectors | Bit | Cables, hubs, repeaters, transceivers |\n\n> **Memory tip:** Layers 1 → 7: **P**lease **D**o **N**ot **T**hrow **S**ausage **P**izza **A**way.\n\n## Key ideas\n\n- **Encapsulation:** going down the stack, each layer wraps the data from the layer above in its own header. Layer 2 also adds a trailer, the FCS used for error checking. The receiver **decapsulates** in reverse order.\n- **Layer 2 sublayers:** LLC (Logical Link Control) faces Layer 3. MAC (Media Access Control) handles hardware addressing and media access.\n- **Addresses by layer:** MAC address = Layer 2; IP address = Layer 3; port number = Layer 4.\n- **Devices by layer:** hub/repeater = 1; switch/bridge/AP = 2; router = 3; Layer 3 switch = 2 and 3; stateful firewall = 3–4; next-gen firewall and Layer 7 load balancer = up to 7.\n\n## TCP/IP model mapping\n\nThe 4-layer TCP/IP model groups OSI layers: **Application** (OSI 5–7), **Transport** (4), **Internet** (3), **Network Access/Link** (1–2).\n\n## Using it to troubleshoot\n\n- **Bottom-up:** start at Layer 1 (link lights, cables) when connectivity is completely down.\n- **Top-down:** start at Layer 7 when one application fails but others work.\n- If ping by IP works but a website errors, Layers 1–3 are fine and the problem is higher up.\n\n> **Exam tip:** CompTIA places encryption (TLS) at **Layer 6**. Know which layer each device and PDU belongs to; those are the most common OSI questions.","1|Networking appliances":"# Networking appliances\n\nKnow what each appliance does, which OSI layer it works at, and when you'd choose it.\n\n## Core devices\n\n| Device | Layer | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| Hub / repeater | 1 | Repeats signals out every port; one shared collision domain |\n| Switch | 2 | Learns MAC addresses and forwards frames only to the right port. Each port is its own collision domain, and a VLAN is one broadcast domain |\n| Router | 3 | Connects different IP networks and forwards packets using a routing table. Each interface is its own broadcast domain |\n| Layer 3 switch | 2–3 | Switching plus routing between VLANs (SVIs) |\n| Wireless access point | 2 | Bridges wireless clients onto the wired LAN |\n| Wireless LAN controller | – | Centrally manages lightweight APs (config, RF, roaming) |\n\n## Security appliances\n\n- **Firewall:** permits or denies traffic between zones using rules.\n  - *Stateless* filters judge each packet on its own.\n  - *Stateful* firewalls track connections and allow return traffic.\n  - *Next-generation (NGFW)* firewalls add application awareness, IPS, and URL filtering.\n- **IDS (intrusion detection system):** passive. It watches a copy of traffic from a SPAN port or TAP and **alerts**.\n- **IPS (intrusion prevention system):** inline. It can **block** traffic, so a false positive drops legitimate traffic.\n- **Detection methods:** *signature-based* catches known attacks; *anomaly-based* compares against a baseline, catching unknown attacks but with more false positives.\n\n## Traffic distribution\n\n- **Load balancer:** spreads requests across a server pool. Methods include round-robin, least connections, and weighted.\n  - Uses **health checks** to skip failed servers.\n  - **Session persistence** (\"sticky sessions\") keeps a user on one server.\n  - Can do **TLS offload**.\n- **Proxy server:** makes requests on behalf of clients.\n  - A **forward proxy** serves internal users, with caching and URL filtering.\n  - A **reverse proxy** sits in front of web servers, protecting and offloading them.\n\n## Storage\n\n| | NAS | SAN |\n|---|---|---|\n| Access | File-level (SMB, NFS) | Block-level (looks like a local disk) |\n| Network | Regular LAN | Dedicated storage network (Fibre Channel, iSCSI, FCoE) |\n\n- **iSCSI** carries SCSI commands over IP.\n- **FCoE** carries Fibre Channel over Ethernet. It isn't routable and needs lossless Ethernet.\n\n## Other appliances\n\n- **VPN concentrator:** terminates many VPN tunnels.\n- **UTM:** all-in-one security box for small offices.\n- **Content delivery network (CDN):** caches content close to users.\n\n> **Exam tip:** IDS = detect and alert (out of band). IPS = detect and **prevent** (inline). Routers break up broadcast domains; switches break up collision domains.","1|Cloud concepts":"# Cloud concepts\n\n## Service models: who manages what\n\n| Model | You manage | Provider manages | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **IaaS** | OS, apps, data, network config | Hardware, virtualization, facility | Virtual machines (EC2, Azure VMs) |\n| **PaaS** | Your application and data | OS, runtime, middleware, and below | App hosting platforms |\n| **SaaS** | Just your data and users | Everything else | Microsoft 365, Gmail |\n\nControl decreases and convenience increases from IaaS → PaaS → SaaS. Under the **shared responsibility model**, the provider always secures the physical data center. The customer always owns their data, identities, and access rules.\n\n## Deployment models\n\n- **Public:** shared, multitenant infrastructure from a provider. Pay as you go.\n- **Private:** dedicated to one organization, on-premises or hosted.\n- **Hybrid:** private and public working together. *Cloud bursting* sends overflow to the public cloud.\n- **Community:** shared by organizations with common needs, such as compliance.\n- **Multicloud:** more than one public provider.\n\n## Cloud networking pieces\n\n- **VPC (virtual private cloud):** your logically isolated network in the cloud, with its own subnets and route tables.\n- **Network security group / security group:** a **stateful** virtual firewall on instances or interfaces. Return traffic is automatic.\n- **Network ACL:** a **stateless** subnet-level filter. Return traffic must be allowed explicitly.\n- **Internet gateway:** two-way internet access for public subnets. A subnet is \"public\" because its route table sends 0.0.0.0/0 to the internet gateway.\n- **NAT gateway:** lets private subnets reach the internet outbound only.\n- **VPN gateway / Direct Connect / ExpressRoute:**\n  - A VPN gateway gives an encrypted tunnel over the internet.\n  - Direct Connect and ExpressRoute are private dedicated links.\n- **VPC peering / transit gateway:** connect VPCs. Peering is not transitive; a transit gateway acts as a hub.\n\n## NFV\n\n**Network functions virtualization** runs routers, firewalls, and load balancers as software (**VNFs**) on standard servers instead of dedicated appliances.\n\n## Cloud characteristics\n\n- **Elasticity:** resources scale up and down automatically with demand.\n- **Multitenancy:** many customers share hardware but are logically isolated.\n- **Metered service:** you pay for what you use, an operating expense rather than a capital one.\n- **Availability zones:** separate data centers within a region, for high availability.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Customer patches the OS\" → IaaS. \"Developers just deploy code\" → PaaS. \"Use the finished app in a browser\" → SaaS. Security groups are stateful; NACLs are stateless.","1|Ports & protocols":"# Ports & protocols\n\nPort numbers identify which application on a host should receive traffic (Layer 4).\n\n**Port ranges:**\n- **Well-known:** 0–1023\n- **Registered:** 1024–49151\n- **Dynamic/ephemeral:** 49152–65535 (clients use these as source ports)\n\n## Ports you must know (N10-009)\n\n| Port | Protocol | Transport | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 20/21 | FTP | TCP | File transfer, unencrypted (21 control, 20 active data) |\n| 22 | SSH / SFTP | TCP | Encrypted remote CLI / file transfer |\n| 23 | Telnet | TCP | Unencrypted remote CLI, replaced by SSH |\n| 25 | SMTP | TCP | Send/relay email between servers |\n| 53 | DNS | UDP and TCP | Name resolution (TCP for zone transfers) |\n| 67/68 | DHCP | UDP | Address assignment (67 server, 68 client) |\n| 69 | TFTP | UDP | Simple file transfer, no authentication |\n| 80 | HTTP | TCP | Web, unencrypted |\n| 123 | NTP | UDP | Time synchronization |\n| 161/162 | SNMP | UDP | Device monitoring (161 polls, 162 traps) |\n| 389 | LDAP | TCP/UDP | Directory queries |\n| 443 | HTTPS | TCP | Web over TLS |\n| 445 | SMB | TCP | Windows file and printer sharing |\n| 514 | Syslog | UDP | Central logging |\n| 587 (465) | SMTPS | TCP | Secure mail submission |\n| 636 | LDAPS | TCP | LDAP over TLS |\n| 1433 | SQL Server | TCP | Microsoft SQL Server |\n| 3389 | RDP | TCP/UDP | Remote desktop |\n| 5060/5061 | SIP | TCP/UDP | VoIP call signaling (5061 = TLS) |\n\n## IP protocol types\n\nThese are protocol numbers in the IP header, not ports:\n\n| Number | Protocol | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | ICMP | Ping, error messages |\n| 6 | TCP | Reliable transport |\n| 17 | UDP | Connectionless transport |\n| 47 | GRE | Unencrypted tunneling |\n| 50 | ESP | IPsec encryption |\n| 51 | AH | IPsec authentication only |\n\nIPsec's **IKE** negotiates keys on UDP 500, and on UDP 4500 when NAT traversal is needed.\n\n## TCP vs UDP\n\n- **TCP:** connection-oriented. Three-way handshake (**SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK**), sequencing, acknowledgments, retransmission, and flow control. Closes with FIN, or aborts with RST.\n- **UDP:** connectionless with low overhead. No guarantees, so it suits voice, video, DNS queries, DHCP, and TFTP.\n\n## Secure replacements\n\nTelnet → **SSH**; HTTP → **HTTPS**; FTP → **SFTP** or FTPS; LDAP → **LDAPS**; SMTP → **SMTPS**; SNMPv1/v2c → **SNMPv3**.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Use the **Ports & protocols drill** to memorize these in both directions. Expect \"which port should be opened\" questions.","1|Traffic types":"# Traffic types\n\nHow many receivers a packet is meant for:\n\n| Type | Delivery | IPv4 example | IPv6? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Unicast** | One sender → one receiver | Normal web or SSH traffic | Yes |\n| **Broadcast** | One → **all** hosts on the local segment | 255.255.255.255, MAC FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF | **No** |\n| **Multicast** | One → a **group** of interested hosts | 224.0.0.0 – 239.255.255.255 | Yes (FF00::/8) |\n| **Anycast** | One → the **nearest** of several hosts sharing an address | DNS root servers, CDNs | Yes |\n\n## Broadcast\n\n- Reaches every device in the **broadcast domain**. Routers don't forward it, but switches do (within a VLAN).\n- Used by **ARP requests** and **DHCP Discover**. A client with no IP sends from 0.0.0.0 to 255.255.255.255.\n- Too much broadcast traffic slows every host. Segment large networks with **VLANs**.\n- A *directed broadcast* (e.g. 192.168.5.255) targets a remote subnet. Routers block these by default because of Smurf attacks.\n\n## Multicast\n\n- Sends one stream that the network copies only where needed. It's efficient for IPTV, video streaming, and routing protocol updates. OSPF uses 224.0.0.5 and 224.0.0.6.\n- Hosts join groups with **IGMP**.\n- **IGMP snooping** on switches forwards multicast only to ports that want it. Without it, multicast floods like broadcast.\n- IPv4 multicast MAC addresses begin **01:00:5E**.\n\n## Anycast\n\n- The same IP is advertised from many locations (via BGP), and routing delivers to the **closest** one. This gives low latency and resilience.\n- Examples: public DNS resolvers such as 1.1.1.1 and 8.8.8.8, and CDN edges.\n\n## IPv6 note\n\nIPv6 has **no broadcast**. It uses multicast groups such as **FF02::1** (all nodes) and **FF02::2** (all routers), plus anycast.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Most efficient way to stream one video to many viewers\" → **multicast**. \"Traffic goes to the closest server with that address\" → **anycast**. \"Not supported in IPv6\" → **broadcast**.","1|Transmission media":"# Transmission media\n\n## Copper twisted pair\n\nPairs are twisted to cancel **EMI** and reduce **crosstalk**. The maximum Ethernet run is **100 m**: 90 m permanent link plus 10 m of patch cords.\n\n| Category | Typical max speed | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cat 5e | 1 Gbps | 100 m |\n| Cat 6 | 10 Gbps up to ~55 m | 1 Gbps to 100 m |\n| Cat 6a | 10 Gbps | Full 100 m |\n| Cat 8 | 25/40 Gbps | ~30 m, data centers |\n\n- **UTP vs STP:** shielded twisted pair resists EMI but must be grounded.\n- **Wiring:** T568A and T568B. Same standard on both ends = **straight-through**; A on one end and B on the other = **crossover**. Modern ports use **Auto-MDIX**, so either cable works. A **rollover** (console) cable reverses pin order.\n- **Jacket ratings:**\n  - **Plenum** (CMP) for air-handling spaces; low smoke when burned.\n  - **Riser** (CMR) for vertical runs between floors.\n  - **Direct burial** for underground runs.\n\n## Fiber optic\n\nFiber carries light, so it's **immune to EMI**, works over long distances, and is hard to tap.\n\n| | Single-mode (SMF) | Multimode (MMF) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Core | ~9 µm | 50 or 62.5 µm |\n| Light source | Laser (1310/1550 nm) | LED/VCSEL (850 nm) |\n| Distance | Kilometers | Hundreds of meters |\n| Examples | 10GBASE-LR (10 km) | 10GBASE-SR (~300 m on OM3) |\n\n- **Distance limit:** multimode is limited by *modal dispersion* (light takes many paths).\n- **Multimode grades:** OM3 and OM4 are laser-optimized multimode for 10G and faster.\n\n## Coaxial\n\n- **RG-6** (75 Ω) with **F-type** connectors carries cable TV and cable modems.\n- RG-59 is older and thinner. Older Ethernet (10BASE2) used BNC connectors.\n\n## DAC and AOC\n\n- **Direct attach copper (DAC):** twinax with SFP/QSFP ends permanently attached. It's the cheapest, lowest-power option for links of a few meters inside or between racks.\n- **Active optical cable (AOC):** the same idea using fiber, so it reaches longer distances.\n\n## Wireless media\n\n- **802.11 Wi-Fi:** see Wireless devices for bands and standards.\n\n| Wi-Fi | Standard | Bands |\n|---|---|---|\n| Wi-Fi 4 | 802.11n | 2.4 and 5 GHz |\n| Wi-Fi 5 | 802.11ac | 5 GHz |\n| Wi-Fi 6 / 6E | 802.11ax | 2.4/5 GHz; 6E adds 6 GHz |\n| Wi-Fi 7 | 802.11be | 2.4, 5, and 6 GHz |\n\n- **Cellular (4G/5G):** quick to deploy anywhere there's coverage; 5G has lower latency.\n- **Satellite:**\n  - Geostationary satellites (~35,786 km up) have **high latency** (~600 ms round trip).\n  - **LEO** constellations are much lower, with latency in the tens of ms.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Long distance\" or \"near EMI sources (motors, elevators)\" → **fiber**. \"Short in-rack 10/25G link, cheapest\" → **DAC**. Exceeding 100 m on copper causes **attenuation**.","1|Transceivers & connectors":"# Transceivers & connectors\n\n## Copper connectors\n\n| Connector | Used for |\n|---|---|\n| **RJ45** (8P8C) | Twisted-pair Ethernet |\n| **RJ11** (6P2C/6P4C) | Telephone lines, DSL |\n| **F-type** | Coax for cable TV/modems (screws on) |\n| **BNC** | Older coax Ethernet and video (bayonet twist-lock) |\n\n**Tools:**\n- **Crimper** attaches RJ45 plugs.\n- **Punchdown tool** terminates wires into patch panels and keystone jacks (110 blocks).\n\n## Fiber connectors\n\n| Connector | How to recognize it |\n|---|---|\n| **LC** | Small form factor, push-pull latch, 1.25 mm ferrule. The most common on SFPs |\n| **SC** | Square, push-pull snap-in, 2.5 mm ferrule |\n| **ST** | Round metal, bayonet twist-lock (older multimode) |\n| **MPO/MTP** | Multi-fiber (12+ fibers) in one ferrule, for 40/100G parallel optics and trunks |\n\n**Polish types** must match:\n- **UPC** connectors are often blue (single-mode).\n- **APC** connectors are **green** and angled 8°, which lowers back reflection.\n- Mating APC with UPC causes high loss.\n\n## Transceivers\n\nHot-swappable modules let one switch port use different media and distances.\n\n| Form factor | Speed |\n|---|---|\n| GBIC | 1 Gbps (old, large) |\n| SFP | 1 Gbps |\n| SFP+ | 10 Gbps |\n| SFP28 | 25 Gbps |\n| QSFP+ | 40 Gbps (4 × 10G) |\n| QSFP28 | 100 Gbps (4 × 25G) |\n\n- **Both ends must match:** same standard, wavelength, and fiber type. 10GBASE-**SR** (850 nm, multimode) won't link with 10GBASE-**LR** (1310 nm, single-mode).\n- **BiDi** optics send and receive on **one** strand using two wavelengths, sold as complementary pairs.\n- **DOM/DDM** reports optical TX/RX power and temperature, which helps troubleshoot weak links.\n- **Media converters** join different media, such as copper to fiber.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Small push-pull fiber connector → **LC**. Square → **SC**. Twist-lock → **ST**. Many fibers in one → **MPO**. Coax screw-on → **F-type**. \"Link won't come up between SR and LR optics\" → **transceiver mismatch**.","1|Network topologies":"# Network topologies\n\n## Physical and logical topologies\n\n| Topology | Description | Pros / cons |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Star** | Every device connects to a central switch | Easy to manage; the central device is a single point of failure |\n| **Hub and spoke** | WAN star: branches connect to HQ, not to each other | Cheap and simple; branch-to-branch traffic goes through the hub |\n| **Full mesh** | Every node connects to every other node | Maximum redundancy; needs **n(n−1)/2** links |\n| **Partial mesh** | Only critical nodes are meshed | Balances cost and redundancy |\n| **Point-to-point** | A dedicated link between exactly two endpoints | Simple; leased lines, wireless bridges |\n| **Point-to-multipoint** | One central site serves many remotes | Common in wireless |\n| **Hybrid** | A mix of the above | Realistic for most networks |\n| **Bus / ring** (legacy) | Shared backbone or a loop | One break can take down everything |\n\nFor example, 6 sites in a full mesh need 6 × 5 / 2 = **15** links.\n\n## Three-tier hierarchical design (campus)\n\n1. **Core:** fast, redundant backbone. Keep it simple, with no heavy policy.\n2. **Distribution:** aggregates access switches, and applies routing between VLANs, ACLs, and QoS.\n3. **Access:** where end devices connect (switch ports, APs, PoE).\n\n**Collapsed core** merges core and distribution into one layer. It suits small and medium sites.\n\n## Spine and leaf (data center)\n\n- **Leaf** (top-of-rack) switches connect servers. **Every leaf connects to every spine**; spines don't connect to each other.\n- Any server is the same number of hops from any other, giving predictable low latency for **east-west** traffic.\n- Uses **ECMP** to spread traffic across all spines.\n- **Scales out:** add leaves for more ports, add spines for more bandwidth.\n\n## Traffic directions\n\n- **North-south:** traffic entering or leaving the data center.\n- **East-west:** traffic between servers inside it.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Highest redundancy\" → full mesh. \"Branches connect only to HQ\" → hub and spoke. \"Data center, predictable server-to-server latency\" → spine and leaf. \"Small campus combining layers\" → collapsed core.","1|IPv4 addressing":"# IPv4 addressing\n\nAn IPv4 address is **32 bits**, written as four octets (e.g. 192.168.10.25). The **subnet mask** or **CIDR prefix** says how many bits are the network portion.\n\n## Address classes (classful)\n\n| Class | First octet | Leading bits | Default mask | Use |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | 1–126 | 0 | 255.0.0.0 (/8) | Unicast, very large networks |\n| B | 128–191 | 10 | 255.255.0.0 (/16) | Unicast, medium networks |\n| C | 192–223 | 110 | 255.255.255.0 (/24) | Unicast, small networks |\n| D | 224–239 | 1110 | – | **Multicast** |\n| E | 240–255 | 1111 | – | Experimental |\n\n127 is reserved for loopback.\n\n## Public vs private (RFC 1918)\n\nPrivate ranges aren't routed on the internet and need **NAT/PAT** to get online:\n- **10.0.0.0/8** (10.0.0.0 – 10.255.255.255)\n- **172.16.0.0/12** (172.16.0.0 – 172.31.255.255)\n- **192.168.0.0/16** (192.168.0.0 – 192.168.255.255)\n\nEverything else in A–C is public. Watch the edges: 172.32.x.x and 192.169.x.x are **public**.\n\n## Special addresses\n\n- **Loopback:** 127.0.0.0/8 (127.0.0.1). Tests the local TCP/IP stack.\n- **APIPA (link-local):** 169.254.0.0/16. Windows self-assigns one when **DHCP fails**.\n- **0.0.0.0:** \"no address yet\" (DHCP Discover source). As **0.0.0.0/0** it's the default route.\n- **255.255.255.255:** limited broadcast.\n\n## Subnetting essentials\n\n- **Usable hosts** = 2^(host bits) − 2. The network ID and broadcast can't be assigned. Example: a /26 has 6 host bits, so 64 − 2 = **62**.\n- **Block size** = 256 − the mask value in the \"interesting\" octet. Example: /27 has a mask octet of 224, so the block size is **32**. Subnets start at .0, .32, .64, .96…\n- **Network ID** = the start of the block. **Broadcast** = the last address of the block. Usable hosts sit in between.\n  - Example: 192.168.10.77/26 → block size 64 → network **.64**, broadcast **.127**, hosts .65–.126.\n- **Subnets from borrowing b bits** = 2^b. Example: borrowing 3 bits from a /24 gives 8 × /27.\n- **/30** gives 2 usable hosts, a classic fit for router-to-router links. /31 is also allowed on point-to-point links.\n\n## CIDR and VLSM\n\n- **CIDR:** classless prefixes (/27, /22) replace fixed classes and allow **route summarization**. Example: 192.168.0.0/24 through 192.168.3.0/24 summarize to **192.168.0.0/22**.\n- **VLSM:** use **different mask lengths** inside one network, sized to each subnet's needs (e.g. /30 for WAN links, /24 for user VLANs). This saves addresses.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Memorize mask octet values **128, 192, 224, 240, 248, 252, 254, 255** and powers of 2. Then practice in the **Subnetting** and **IP classes** drills until it's automatic.","1|Modern network environments":"# Modern network environments\n\n## SDN (software-defined networking)\n\nSDN separates the network's \"brain\" from the forwarding hardware:\n\n- **Control plane:** decides where traffic goes (routing protocols, tables). In SDN this is centralized in a **controller**.\n- **Data plane:** actually forwards packets.\n- **Management plane:** configuration and monitoring (SSH, SNMP, APIs).\n- **APIs:**\n  - **Northbound** APIs connect the controller to applications and orchestration.\n  - **Southbound** APIs (e.g. OpenFlow) connect the controller to switches.\n\n## SD-WAN\n\nSD-WAN centrally manages several WAN links (MPLS, broadband, LTE/5G) and steers traffic **by application**. It can use cheaper internet links while protecting critical apps, and it builds encrypted overlays automatically.\n\n## VXLAN\n\nVXLAN extends **Layer 2 over a Layer 3** network by wrapping frames in **UDP (port 4789)**.\n- Its 24-bit **VNI** allows about 16 million segments, compared with 4,094 VLANs.\n- **VTEPs** (VXLAN tunnel endpoints) encapsulate and decapsulate the traffic.\n- It adds about 50 bytes, so increase the underlay MTU.\n\n## Zero trust architecture (ZTA)\n\n**Never trust, always verify.** Being \"inside\" the network grants nothing. Every request is checked against identity, device health, and context, with **least privilege** and **micro-segmentation**. **ZTNA** grants access per application instead of placing users on the whole network the way a traditional VPN does.\n\n## SASE and SSE\n\n- **SSE (Security Service Edge):** cloud-delivered security, including a secure web gateway (SWG), CASB, ZTNA, and firewall-as-a-service.\n- **SASE (Secure Access Service Edge):** SSE plus SD-WAN networking, delivered from the cloud.\n\n## Infrastructure as code (IaC)\n\nDefine network and system configuration in version-controlled files and templates, using tools like Ansible or Terraform. The benefits are **repeatable deployments, less configuration drift, peer review, and easy rollback**.\n\n## IPv6 essentials\n\n- **Address size:** 128 bits, written in hex. Shorten it by dropping leading zeros and replacing **one** run of zero groups with `::`. Example: 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:ff00:0042:8329 → **2001:db8::ff00:42:8329**.\n- **Address types:**\n\n| Type | Prefix |\n|---|---|\n| Global unicast (public) | 2000::/3 |\n| Unique local (private) | FC00::/7 (FD00::/8 in practice) |\n| Link-local | FE80::/10 |\n| Multicast | FF00::/8 |\n| Loopback | ::1 |\n\n- **No broadcast:** IPv6 uses multicast and anycast instead.\n- **SLAAC:** hosts build their own address from the router advertisement prefix (using EUI-64 or a random interface ID).\n- **NDP** (Neighbor Discovery Protocol, over ICMPv6) replaces ARP and finds routers.\n- **Transition methods:**\n  - **Dual stack** runs both protocols.\n  - **Tunneling** carries IPv6 inside IPv4.\n  - **NAT64** lets IPv6-only hosts reach IPv4 services.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Steer traffic across multiple WAN links by application\" → SD-WAN. \"Layer 2 over Layer 3, 16 million segments\" → VXLAN. \"Never trust, always verify\" → zero trust.","2|Routing technologies":"# Routing technologies\n\nRouters forward packets between networks using a **routing table**. Each entry has a destination prefix, a next hop or exit interface, an administrative distance, and a metric.\n\n## Static vs dynamic routing\n\n- **Static routes:** entered by hand. Predictable, low overhead, but they don't adapt to failures.\n  - A **default route** (0.0.0.0/0) is the \"gateway of last resort\".\n  - A **floating static route** has a higher administrative distance and acts as a backup.\n- **Dynamic routing protocols:** learn routes automatically and reconverge around failures.\n\n| Protocol | Type | Metric | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| RIP | Distance vector | Hop count (max 15) | Slow and legacy |\n| OSPF | Link state | Cost (from bandwidth) | Areas, with **Area 0** as the backbone; SPF algorithm; IP protocol 89 |\n| EIGRP | Advanced distance vector (hybrid) | Bandwidth + delay | Cisco; DUAL algorithm with feasible successors for fast failover |\n| BGP | Path vector | Path attributes (AS_PATH and others) | Routes **between autonomous systems** (the internet); TCP 179 |\n\n- **IGP vs EGP:** OSPF, EIGRP, and RIP are **interior** gateway protocols (within one organization). BGP is the **exterior** protocol. eBGP runs between ASes and iBGP runs inside one.\n- **Loop prevention:** distance vector uses **split horizon** and route poisoning. BGP rejects routes whose AS_PATH already contains its own AS.\n\n## Route selection (in order)\n\n1. **Longest prefix match.** The most specific route wins: /24 beats /16 beats /0.\n2. **Administrative distance** (lower = more trusted) breaks ties between sources for the same prefix:\n\n| Source | AD |\n|---|---|\n| Connected | 0 |\n| Static | 1 |\n| eBGP | 20 |\n| EIGRP (internal) | 90 |\n| OSPF | 110 |\n| RIP | 120 |\n| iBGP | 200 |\n\n3. **Metric** decides within one protocol. Equal metrics can be load-shared with **ECMP**.\n\n## NAT and PAT\n\n- **Static NAT:** a permanent 1:1 mapping, used for servers that must be reachable.\n- **Dynamic NAT:** maps from a pool of public addresses.\n- **PAT (NAT overload):** many private hosts share **one** public IP, told apart by **source port**. This is how most homes and offices work.\n- **Port forwarding:** a public IP and port map to an inside host and port.\n- **Terminology:** *inside local* = private address; *inside global* = the public address it's translated to.\n\n## FHRP and VIP\n\n**First hop redundancy protocols** give hosts a **virtual IP (VIP)** as their default gateway, shared by two or more routers.\n- **HSRP** (Cisco) uses active and standby routers.\n- **VRRP** (open standard) uses master and backup routers.\n- **GLBP** (Cisco) adds load balancing.\n- **Preemption** lets a higher-priority router take back the active role.\n\n## Subinterfaces\n\n**Router-on-a-stick:** one physical router port carries many VLANs over an 802.1Q trunk, using one **subinterface** per VLAN (e.g. Gi0/0.10 with `encapsulation dot1Q 10`). A Layer 3 switch does the same job with **SVIs**.\n\n> **Exam tip:** First check **prefix length**, then **AD**, then **metric**. \"Many hosts, one public IP\" → PAT. \"Gateway survives a router failure\" → FHRP/VIP.","2|Switching technologies":"# Switching technologies\n\nSwitches learn **source MAC addresses** into a MAC (CAM) table and forward frames only to the right port. Unknown destinations and broadcasts are **flooded** within the VLAN.\n\n## VLANs\n\nA VLAN is a separate **broadcast domain** on shared switch hardware. VLANs improve security and performance, and need a **Layer 3 device** to route between them.\n\n- **Access port:** belongs to one data VLAN and sends untagged frames to the end device. It can add a **voice VLAN** for an IP phone, which learns that VLAN via CDP or LLDP-MED.\n- **Trunk port:** carries many VLANs between switches using **802.1Q tags**.\n  - The tag is 4 bytes and holds a 12-bit VLAN ID (usable IDs 1–4094) and 802.1p priority (CoS).\n  - The **native VLAN** is sent **untagged**. It must match on both ends, and best practice is an unused VLAN, not VLAN 1, to prevent VLAN hopping.\n  - Limit the **allowed VLAN list** to what's needed.\n- **Hardening:** hard-code access or trunk mode and disable **DTP** (prevents switch spoofing).\n\n## Interface configuration\n\n- **Speed and duplex:** set both sides to auto, or hard-code both identically.\n  - A **duplex mismatch** gives late collisions and CRC errors, and slow throughput.\n  - Fixed on one side with auto on the other → the auto side falls back to **half duplex**.\n- **Link aggregation (802.3ad/LACP):** bundles links into one logical port channel for bandwidth and redundancy. Speed, duplex, and VLAN settings must match on all members. LACP modes: active–active or active–passive works; passive–passive doesn't.\n- **Port security:** limits the number of MAC addresses on a port. Violation actions are shutdown, restrict, or protect.\n- **Descriptions and unused ports:** label ports with descriptions; shut down unused ports and put them in a parking VLAN.\n\n## Spanning Tree Protocol (STP)\n\nRedundant Layer 2 paths cause **loops**, which lead to broadcast storms, MAC table flapping, and duplicate frames. Ethernet has no TTL to stop them. **STP (802.1D)** blocks redundant paths.\n\n1. Elect a **root bridge**: lowest **bridge ID** (priority, default **32768**, then MAC address). Set the priority deliberately; otherwise the oldest switch may win.\n2. Each non-root switch picks a **root port** (its best path to the root).\n3. Each segment gets a **designated port**. Everything else **blocks**.\n\n- **Port states (classic STP):** blocking → listening → learning → forwarding (about 30–50 seconds).\n- **RSTP (802.1w):** converges in seconds.\n- **PVST+ / MSTP (802.1s):** spanning-tree instances per VLAN or per group of VLANs.\n- **Protection features:**\n  - **PortFast** skips straight to forwarding on edge (host) ports.\n  - **BPDU guard** err-disables an edge port that receives a BPDU.\n  - **Root guard** stops a port from becoming the path to a new root.\n\n## MTU and jumbo frames\n\n- **Standard Ethernet MTU:** **1500 bytes** of payload (1518-byte frame, 1522 with a tag).\n- **Jumbo frames:** about **9000 bytes**. They reduce overhead for storage traffic (iSCSI/NFS) and backups.\n- **Every device in the path must support the larger MTU.** Otherwise oversized frames are dropped and show up as **giants**.\n- **Tunnels:** VXLAN and other overlays need a larger underlay MTU.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Prevent switching loops\" → STP. \"Native VLAN mismatch\" → traffic leaks between VLANs. \"Combine links\" → LACP. \"Storage network performance\" → jumbo frames end to end.","2|Wireless devices":"# Wireless devices\n\n## Frequency bands and channels\n\n| Band | Range and penetration | Channels | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **2.4 GHz** | Longest | Only **1, 6, 11** don't overlap (20 MHz) | Crowded; microwaves and Bluetooth interfere |\n| **5 GHz** | Medium | Many non-overlapping channels | **DFS** channels must give way to radar |\n| **6 GHz** | Shortest | Lots of clean spectrum | Wi-Fi 6E/7 clients only |\n\n- **Channel width:** wider channels (40/80/160 MHz) are faster per client but give fewer non-overlapping channels. Dense deployments often use 20/40 MHz.\n- **Co-channel interference:** APs on the **same** channel share airtime.\n- **Adjacent-channel interference:** **overlapping** channels (e.g. 3 and 5) corrupt each other.\n- **Band steering:** nudges dual-band clients to 5/6 GHz.\n\n## 802.11 standards\n\n| Name | Standard | Bands | Key features |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| — | 802.11a / b / g | 5 / 2.4 / 2.4 GHz | Legacy |\n| Wi-Fi 4 | 802.11n | 2.4 and 5 GHz | MIMO |\n| Wi-Fi 5 | 802.11ac | 5 GHz | Downlink MU-MIMO, wider channels |\n| Wi-Fi 6 / 6E | 802.11ax | 2.4/5 (+6 for 6E) | **OFDMA**, uplink and downlink MU-MIMO, Target Wake Time |\n| Wi-Fi 7 | 802.11be | 2.4/5/6 | 320 MHz channels, multi-link operation |\n\n## Network types\n\n- **Infrastructure:** clients connect through APs. A **BSS** is one AP; an **ESS** is many APs sharing one SSID so clients can roam.\n- **Ad hoc (IBSS):** client to client with no AP.\n- **Mesh:** APs relay traffic wirelessly to each other.\n- **Point-to-point and point-to-multipoint bridges:** link buildings.\n- **Identifiers:** the **SSID** is the network name; the **BSSID** is the AP radio's MAC. Hiding the SSID is **not** real security.\n\n## Encryption and authentication\n\n| Option | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| WEP / WPA-TKIP | Broken; never use |\n| **WPA2** | AES-CCMP; Personal (PSK) or Enterprise (802.1X) |\n| **WPA3-Personal** | **SAE** replaces the PSK handshake and resists offline dictionary attacks |\n| **WPA3-Enterprise** | 802.1X; optional 192-bit mode |\n| **Enhanced Open (OWE)** | Encrypts open networks without a password |\n\n- **Enterprise (802.1X/EAP + RADIUS):** each user has their own credentials.\n  - **EAP-TLS** uses certificates on both client and server, and is the strongest.\n  - **PEAP** uses a server certificate plus a password (MSCHAPv2) inside a tunnel.\n- **Personal (PSK):** one shared passphrase.\n- **Disable WPS PIN:** it can be brute-forced.\n- **MAC filtering** is weak because MAC addresses can be spoofed.\n- **802.11w (PMF)** protects management frames against deauthentication attacks.\n\n## Guest networks\n\nPut guests on a **separate SSID and VLAN** with **internet-only** firewall rules and **client isolation**. Optionally add a **captive portal** for terms or login.\n\n## Antennas and APs\n\n- **Omnidirectional:** 360° coverage.\n- **Directional** (Yagi, parabolic): long, narrow links. **Patch/panel** antennas are semi-directional, wall-mounted.\n- **Gain** is measured in dBi. **EIRP** = transmit power + antenna gain − cable loss, and regulations cap it.\n- **Autonomous (thick) APs** hold their own configuration. **Lightweight (thin) APs** are managed by a **wireless LAN controller** over CAPWAP, which also handles RF management (channels and power).\n- **Roaming:** 802.11k gives neighbor lists, 802.11v gives roaming hints, and **802.11r** gives fast transition.\n- **Site surveys:** **predictive** (modeled), **passive** (listen), and **active** (connect and measure throughput).\n\n> **Exam tip:** 2.4 GHz → channels 1/6/11. \"Strongest home Wi-Fi security\" → WPA3-Personal (SAE). \"Each employee logs in with directory credentials\" → WPA2/3-Enterprise with 802.1X/RADIUS.","2|Physical installations":"# Physical installations\n\n## Where equipment lives\n\n- **MDF (main distribution frame):** the central wiring point. It usually houses the core equipment and the provider's **demarc**, where carrier responsibility ends and yours begins.\n- **IDF (intermediate distribution frame):** a closet serving a floor or area. It holds access switches and patch panels, and uplinks to the MDF. Place it so **horizontal runs stay within 90 m**.\n- **Racks:**\n  - Standard width is **19 inches**, measured in **rack units** (1U = 1.75 in).\n  - **Open-frame** racks (2-post or 4-post) suit secured rooms.\n  - **Locking enclosed cabinets** add physical security.\n- **Rack practice:**\n  - Heaviest items (UPS batteries) go at the **bottom**.\n  - Fit **blanking panels** in empty spaces.\n  - Use cable managers and **hook-and-loop** straps, not tight zip ties.\n  - **Label both ends** of every cable.\n  - Ground and bond the racks.\n\n## Power\n\n| Device | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| **UPS** | Immediate battery power during outages and power conditioning; bridges the gap until the generator starts |\n| **Generator** | Long-term power during extended outages |\n| **PDU** | Distributes power within a rack; *managed* PDUs report per-outlet use and can power-cycle remotely |\n| **Surge protector** | Spike protection only; no runtime |\n\n- **UPS types:** **online (double-conversion)** gives the cleanest power with zero transfer time. Line-interactive and standby UPSs switch to battery only when needed.\n- **Sizing:** size in **VA and watts**, with headroom. Keep continuous load at **≤80%** of the circuit rating.\n- **Redundancy:** connect dual power supplies to **separate PDUs and circuits** (A/B feeds).\n- **Voltage:** about 120 V in North America and about 230 V in Europe. Telecom equipment often uses **−48 V DC**.\n- **PoE:**\n  - **802.3af** supplies about 15.4 W, **802.3at (PoE+)** about 30 W, and **802.3bt** about 60–100 W.\n  - The switch has a **total PoE budget**.\n  - A PoE **injector** adds power to a single link.\n\n## Environment\n\n- **Temperature:** about **18–27 °C (64–81 °F)** at equipment intakes (ASHRAE).\n- **Humidity:** about **40–60%**. Too low causes **static discharge (ESD)**; too high causes **condensation and corrosion**.\n- **Hot aisle / cold aisle:** racks face each other so intakes share a cold aisle and exhausts share a hot aisle. Match device **airflow direction** to the layout.\n- **Fire suppression:** **clean-agent** gas (e.g. FM-200, inert gas) puts out fires without damaging electronics. **Pre-action** sprinklers keep pipes dry until a fire is detected.\n- **Monitoring:** temperature, humidity, water-leak, and smoke sensors.\n- **Cabling:**\n  - Use **plenum**-rated cable in air-handling spaces, **riser** cable between floors, and **direct-burial** cable outdoors.\n  - Keep data cables away from power cables (EMI).\n  - Respect **bend radius** (about 4× the diameter for UTP).\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Short outage, graceful shutdown\" → UPS. \"Hours-long outage\" → generator. \"Low humidity risk\" → ESD. \"Above a drop ceiling\" → plenum cable.","3|Documentation":"# Documentation\n\nGood documentation makes troubleshooting, onboarding, audits, and change planning faster. It's only useful if it's **updated as part of every change**.\n\n## Diagrams\n\n| Type | Shows |\n|---|---|\n| **Physical diagram** | Actual devices, ports, cable runs, and locations |\n| **Logical diagram** | IP subnets, VLANs, routing domains, and how traffic flows |\n| **Rack diagram (elevation)** | What sits in each rack unit, front and back, including PDUs and patch panels |\n| **Cable map / port map** | Which wall jack connects to which patch panel port and switch port |\n| **Network diagram** | General term for any of the above; use a consistent legend and symbols |\n\n## Records and inventories\n\n- **Asset inventory:** hardware and software records, including model, **serial number**, firmware version, location, owner, warranty/support dates, and licenses. You can't protect or plan for what you don't know about.\n- **IPAM (IP address management):** tracks subnets, static assignments, DHCP scopes, and DNS records. It prevents **overlaps and duplicate IPs** and shows when scopes are running out.\n- **Baselines:** normal performance levels (utilization, latency, errors), so you can spot anomalies.\n- **SOPs:** step-by-step procedures for routine tasks.\n\n## Agreements\n\n| Document | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| **SLA** (service level agreement) | Measurable commitments: uptime %, response and repair times, penalties |\n| **MOU** (memorandum of understanding) | Non-binding statement of shared intent |\n| **NDA** | Legally protects confidential information |\n| **SOW** (statement of work) | Specific tasks, deliverables, and timeline of a project |\n| **AUP** (acceptable use policy) | What users may and may not do on company systems |\n\n**Uptime math:**\n- 99.9% ≈ **8.76 hours** of downtime per year\n- 99.99% ≈ **52.6 minutes** per year\n- 99.999% (\"five nines\") ≈ **5.26 minutes** per year\n\n## Wireless surveys\n\n- **Predictive:** software models coverage from floor plans *before* installing.\n- **Passive:** walk the site and listen to existing APs (signal, noise, channels).\n- **Active:** connect and measure real throughput, latency, and roaming.\n- **Heat maps** visualize signal strength across the floor plan.\n- **Re-survey** after renovations or major changes.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Which document shows VLANs and subnets?\" → logical diagram. \"Which shows what's in each U of a rack?\" → rack diagram. \"Uptime guarantee\" → SLA.","3|Life-cycle management":"# Life-cycle management\n\nEvery device moves through **procure → deploy → operate/maintain → decommission/dispose**. Managing that life cycle keeps the network supported, patched, and budgeted.\n\n## Vendor milestones\n\n| Term | Meaning | Risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| **End of sale / EOL (end of life)** | The vendor stops selling the product and starts phasing it out | Plan replacements; spares get harder to find |\n| **EOS (end of support)** | No more patches, bug fixes, or technical support | **Security risk**: new vulnerabilities won't be fixed |\n\nTrack these dates in the **asset inventory** so refreshes can be budgeted *before* devices go unsupported. If an EOS device has an unpatchable vulnerability, compensating controls (such as ACLs) only reduce risk short-term. **Replace it**.\n\n## Software management\n\n- **Read the release notes** first: known issues, required upgrade paths, feature changes.\n- **Back up** the current configuration and image before upgrading.\n- **Verify the file hash** (e.g. SHA-256) against the vendor's published value to confirm integrity.\n- **Test in a lab**, then deploy through **change management** in a **maintenance window**, with a **rollback plan**.\n- **Standardize versions** across devices. Vendors' \"recommended\" releases are usually more stable than the newest one.\n- **Track licenses and subscriptions.** An expired license can disable features or security updates.\n\n## Decommissioning\n\n1. **Wipe or sanitize** configurations and storage (they hold credentials, SNMP strings, and topology details). For sensitive media, **destroy** it and keep a **certificate of destruction**.\n2. **Remove leftovers:** accounts, certificates, firewall rules, VPN tunnels, and monitoring entries tied to the device.\n3. **Update records:** asset inventory, diagrams, IPAM, and documentation.\n\n> **Exam tip:** EOL = can't buy it. **EOS = no more security patches**, and that's the one that creates risk. Always back up and have a rollback plan before upgrades.","3|Change management":"# Change management\n\nChange management controls **how changes are requested, reviewed, approved, scheduled, carried out, and documented**. The goal is fewer unplanned outages and a clear record of what changed.\n\n## The request process\n\n1. **Submit a change request:** what, why (justification), affected systems, risk and impact, schedule, **test plan**, and **rollback (backout) plan**.\n2. **Assess risk and impact:** who and what could be affected?\n3. **Approval:** significant changes go to the **change advisory board (CAB)**.\n4. **Schedule** in a **maintenance window** and **notify** affected users.\n5. **Implement**, ideally after testing in a lab or staging environment.\n6. **Verify** the change worked. If it didn't, **roll back**.\n7. **Document** the outcome and update records. Hold a **post-implementation review** for lessons learned.\n\n## Types of change\n\n| Type | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Standard** | Low-risk, routine, pre-approved with a documented procedure (e.g. moving a user port to a VLAN) |\n| **Normal** | Goes through full review and CAB approval |\n| **Emergency** | Urgent (outage, critical vulnerability). Fast-tracked approval, but still documented and reviewed afterward |\n\n## Other terms\n\n- **Change freeze:** a period, such as year-end, when non-emergency changes aren't allowed.\n- **Unauthorized change:** anything made outside the process. It's a risk and may indicate a policy violation or compromise.\n- **Tracking:** a ticketing system and version-controlled configs provide an **audit trail**.\n\n## Why it matters for troubleshooting\n\nOne of the first troubleshooting questions is **\"What changed?\"** Change records answer it quickly, because many outages follow recent changes.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Every change request needs a **rollback plan**. Emergency changes skip the wait, not the documentation.","3|Configuration management":"# Configuration management\n\n## Three kinds of configuration\n\n- **Production configuration:** what's running on the device and serving live traffic. Change it only through change management.\n- **Backup configuration:** saved copies used to restore after a failure or bad change. Back up **automatically on a schedule**, keep several versions, and **test restores**.\n- **Baseline (golden) configuration:** the approved standard for a device role. New devices are built from it and running configs are compared against it.\n\n## Running vs startup config\n\nOn most network devices:\n- The **running configuration** lives in memory and is active now.\n- The **startup configuration** lives in NVRAM and loads at boot.\n\nUnsaved changes **disappear after a reboot**. Save with e.g. `copy running-config startup-config`.\n\n## Configuration drift\n\n**Drift** is when devices slowly diverge from the baseline through manual tweaks and forgotten emergency fixes. Detect it with **configuration management/compliance tools** that diff running configs against the baseline. Investigate unexplained differences: they could be unauthorized changes.\n\n## Good practices\n\n- **Storage:** keep backups **off the device**, in an access-controlled location with versioning (e.g. a Git repository).\n- **Transfer:** move configs with **SCP or SFTP**, not TFTP or FTP.\n- **Secrets:** never store passwords in plain text. Use hashed secrets and a password vault.\n- **Consistency:** use **templates and automation** (infrastructure as code, Ansible) for consistent, hardened deployments. **Zero-touch provisioning** configures new devices automatically on first boot.\n- **Troubleshooting:** **diff** the current config against the last known-good backup.\n- **Safe changes:** commit-confirm or rollback timers revert a change automatically if you lose access.\n- **Inventory:** a **CMDB** records configuration items and their dependencies, which helps assess change impact.\n- **Time:** configure **NTP** so logs and change records have accurate timestamps.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Changes lost after reboot\" → running config wasn't saved. \"Devices no longer match the standard\" → configuration drift; compare against the baseline.","3|Network monitoring":"# Network monitoring\n\n## SNMP\n\n**SNMP** lets a **manager** (NMS) collect data from **agents** on devices.\n- Each value is an **OID**, defined in a **MIB**.\n- The manager **polls** with Get/GetNext/GetBulk/Walk on **UDP 161** and changes values with **Set**.\n- Agents send unsolicited **traps** to the manager on **UDP 162**. **Informs** are traps that require an acknowledgment.\n\n| Version | Security |\n|---|---|\n| v1 / v2c | Community strings in **cleartext** (\"public\"/\"private\" are well-known defaults) |\n| **v3** | Users with **authentication and encryption** (authPriv) |\n\n## Flow data\n\n**NetFlow / IPFIX / sFlow** export conversation summaries: source and destination IP, ports, protocol, bytes, and time. Use them to find **top talkers**, plan capacity, and spot unusual traffic. **sFlow samples** packets, which keeps overhead low on fast links.\n\n## Packet capture\n\nFull packets (headers and payload) for deep analysis with **Wireshark** or **tcpdump**. Captures need a lot of storage and can't read encrypted payloads.\n\nGetting the traffic:\n- **Port mirroring (SPAN):** the switch copies traffic to a monitor port. It can drop frames if oversubscribed. **RSPAN/ERSPAN** send the copy to another switch.\n- **Network TAP:** a hardware device that passively copies everything, including errors.\n\n## Baselines and metrics\n\nRecord what's normal so problems stand out:\n- **Utilization:** compare against bandwidth vs **throughput** (what's actually achieved).\n- **Latency, jitter, packet loss.**\n- **Interface errors and discards:** CRC errors point to Layer 1 problems; discards usually mean congestion.\n- **CPU, memory, and up/down status.**\n\nSet **thresholds and alerts** on these.\n\n## Logs\n\n- **Syslog** (UDP 514) sends device logs to a central server. Severity levels run from 0 to 7:\n\n| Level | Name | Level | Name |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | Emergency | 4 | Warning |\n| 1 | Alert | 5 | Notice |\n| 2 | Critical | 6 | Informational |\n| 3 | Error | 7 | Debug |\n\nSetting a level sends that level and everything more severe.\n- **Log aggregation / SIEM:** centralizes and **correlates** logs across devices, alerts on security events, and retains logs for compliance. Accurate **NTP** time is essential.\n\n## API integration\n\nModern tools exchange data through **REST APIs** and **webhooks**: monitoring can open tickets, enrich alerts, or trigger automation. **Streaming telemetry** pushes data from devices instead of waiting to be polled.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Who's using the bandwidth?\" → NetFlow. \"See the actual packets\" → packet capture via SPAN or a TAP. \"Secure SNMP\" → SNMPv3.","3|Disaster recovery":"# Disaster recovery\n\n## The four metrics\n\n| Metric | Meaning | Lower or higher is better? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **RPO** (recovery point objective) | Maximum acceptable **data loss**, measured in time back to the last good copy | Lower. Driven by backup and replication frequency |\n| **RTO** (recovery time objective) | Maximum acceptable **downtime** before service must be restored | Lower. Driven by the recovery method and site type |\n| **MTTR** (mean time to repair) | Average time to fix a failure | Lower. Spares, documentation, and support contracts help |\n| **MTBF** (mean time between failures) | Average uptime between failures | **Higher** = more reliable |\n\nExample: backups every 6 hours → worst case you lose **6 hours** of data (RPO). Near-zero RPO needs **synchronous replication**.\n\n## Recovery sites\n\n| Site | Ready state | Recovery time | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Hot** | Fully equipped with current data | Minutes | Highest |\n| **Warm** | Hardware in place; data must be restored | Hours to days | Medium |\n| **Cold** | Space, power, and cooling only | Days to weeks | Lowest |\n\nCloud **DRaaS** can provide hot or warm capacity without a second data center. Keep sites far enough apart (**geographic diversity**) and use **diverse ISPs**.\n\n## High availability designs\n\n- **Active-active:** all nodes carry traffic at once. Each must stay under about 50% load so one can absorb the other's traffic if a node fails.\n- **Active-passive:** a standby node takes over on failure. Simpler, but idle capacity.\n- **Redundancy:**\n  - **N+1** means one more component than needed.\n  - Redundant power supplies, links, and **clustering**.\n  - Remove **single points of failure**.\n\n## Backups\n\n- **Full:** everything.\n- **Incremental:** changes since the **last backup of any type**. Fast to back up; a restore needs the full plus every incremental.\n- **Differential:** changes since the **last full**. A restore needs the full plus the latest differential.\n- **3-2-1 rule:** 3 copies, on 2 different media, with 1 offsite. Keep an **offline or immutable** copy to survive ransomware.\n- **Snapshots** are quick rollback points, not a replacement for backups.\n\n## Testing the plan\n\n| Test | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| **Tabletop exercise** | Team talks through the plan; no systems touched |\n| **Walkthrough / checklist** | Review steps and contacts |\n| **Parallel test** | DR systems run alongside production |\n| **Full interruption / failover** | Production actually fails over. Most realistic, highest risk |\n\nTest regularly, at least annually and after major changes. Capture lessons in an **after-action report**.\n\n> **Exam tip:** RP**O** = how much data you can lose (**O**ops, data gone). RT**O** = how long you can be down (**T**ime). \"Talk through it\" → tabletop.","3|Network services":"# Network services\n\n## DHCP\n\nDHCP automatically gives clients an IP address, mask, gateway, DNS servers, and other settings.\n\n- **DORA:**\n  1. Client broadcasts **Discover** (from 0.0.0.0 to 255.255.255.255).\n  2. Server sends an **Offer**.\n  3. Client sends a **Request**.\n  4. Server sends an **Acknowledge**.\n- **Ports:** UDP **67** (server) and **68** (client).\n- **Scope:** the address pool and options for one subnet.\n  - **Exclusions** are addresses in the scope that are never handed out.\n  - **Reservations** always give the same IP to a given MAC address.\n- **Options:** **3** = default gateway, **6** = DNS servers, **15** = domain name, **66/150** = TFTP server for phones.\n- **Lease:** clients renew at 50% (T1) and rebind at 87.5% (T2). Short leases help busy guest networks avoid **pool exhaustion**.\n- **Relay (IP helper):** routers don't forward broadcasts, so a relay on the client's gateway forwards DHCP to a server on another subnet.\n- **High availability:** DHCP **failover** pairs or split scopes.\n- **No server reachable:** Windows clients self-assign an **APIPA** address (169.254.x.x).\n\n## SLAAC (IPv6)\n\n**Stateless address autoconfiguration:** hosts build their own address from the **router advertisement** prefix plus an interface ID (EUI-64 or random), with no server needed.\n- **Stateless DHCPv6** can add extras such as DNS.\n- **Stateful DHCPv6** assigns the addresses themselves. The router advertisement **M flag** says \"use DHCPv6 for addresses\"; the **O flag** says \"get other settings\".\n\n## DNS\n\nDNS resolves names to IP addresses on port **53** (UDP for queries, TCP for zone transfers).\n\n| Record | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| **A** | Name → IPv4 |\n| **AAAA** | Name → IPv6 |\n| **CNAME** | Alias → canonical name |\n| **MX** | Mail server for a domain (lowest preference wins) |\n| **PTR** | IP → name (reverse lookup) |\n| **NS** | Authoritative name servers for a zone |\n| **SOA** | Zone's primary server, serial number, timers |\n| **SRV** | Host and port for a service (e.g. LDAP, SIP) |\n| **TXT** | Text: SPF, DKIM, DMARC, domain verification |\n\n- **Recursive resolver vs authoritative server:** a resolver finds answers for clients by asking root → TLD → authoritative servers; an authoritative server holds the actual records.\n- **TTL:** how long answers are cached. Lower it before a migration.\n- **Zone transfers:** copy a zone from primary to secondary servers. **Restrict them**, because they reveal every host.\n- **Split-horizon DNS:** different answers for internal and external clients.\n- **Security:**\n  - **DNSSEC** signs records, which prevents spoofing and cache poisoning.\n  - **DoT** (TCP **853**) and **DoH** (443) encrypt queries for privacy.\n\n## Time: NTP, PTP, NTS\n\n- **NTP** (UDP 123) synchronizes clocks.\n  - **Stratum 0** is the reference clock (GPS, atomic); **stratum 1** servers attach directly to it.\n  - Configure several servers.\n- **NTS (Network Time Security)** adds cryptographic authentication to NTP, so time can't be spoofed.\n- **PTP (IEEE 1588)** uses hardware timestamps for sub-microsecond accuracy (finance, telecom, industrial).\n- **Why time matters:** log correlation, **Kerberos** (default 5-minute skew limit), and certificate validity all depend on accurate time.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Clients on another subnet get no DHCP address\" → missing **relay/IP helper**. \"Mail server record\" → MX. \"Reverse lookup\" → PTR. \"Secure, authenticated time\" → NTS.","3|Access & management":"# Access & management\n\n## VPNs\n\n| Type | Use |\n|---|---|\n| **Site-to-site** | Permanent tunnel joining two networks (branch ↔ HQ) via gateways; users need no client |\n| **Client-to-site (remote access)** | One device tunnels into the corporate network using VPN client software |\n| **Clientless** | Browser-based TLS portal to specific internal web apps |\n\n- **Full tunnel:** *all* traffic goes through the VPN for corporate inspection and filtering.\n- **Split tunnel:** only corporate-bound traffic uses the VPN. It saves bandwidth but loses visibility of internet traffic.\n- **Protocols:**\n  - **IPsec**: IKE on UDP 500/4500, then ESP for encryption. **Tunnel mode** wraps the whole packet (site-to-site); **transport mode** protects only the payload.\n  - **SSL/TLS VPN** on TCP 443 passes through firewalls easily.\n  - **WireGuard** is modern, fast, and UDP-based.\n  - **L2TP** needs IPsec for encryption. **PPTP** is insecure.\n- **Site-to-site IPsec:** encryption and hash algorithms, the authentication method (PSK or certificates), and the DH group **must match** on both ends.\n\n## Management methods\n\n| Method | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| **SSH** (TCP 22) | Encrypted CLI. Use **v2 only**, key-based authentication, and restrict VTY lines with ACLs. Never Telnet |\n| **GUI** | Web interface. **HTTPS only**, reachable only from management networks |\n| **API** | Programmatic access (REST with JSON/XML, NETCONF on 830 with YANG models). Authenticate with tokens or keys over HTTPS, least privilege |\n| **Console** | Direct serial access via a **rollover/USB console cable** (classically 9600 baud, 8-N-1). Works with no IP configuration |\n\n## In-band vs out-of-band\n\n- **In-band:** manage devices over the production network. Convenient, but unreachable during outages.\n- **Out-of-band (OOB):** a separate path, such as a **console server**, a dedicated management network, or a **cellular modem**. It lets you reach devices when the production network is down.\n\n## Securing administrative access\n\n- A **dedicated management VLAN or network** separates management traffic from user traffic.\n- A **jump box (bastion host)** is a hardened, monitored entry point into secure zones.\n- **Central AAA** with **TACACS+** gives per-command authorization and accounting. Keep a vaulted **local break-glass account** for when AAA servers are unreachable.\n- Add **MFA** on VPN and admin logins.\n- Don't expose **RDP (3389)** directly to the internet. Use a VPN or an RD gateway.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Reach the router when the WAN is down\" → out-of-band (console server or cellular). \"Only corporate traffic in the tunnel\" → split tunnel. \"Replace Telnet\" → SSH.","4|Logical security":"# Logical security\n\n## Encryption\n\n- **Data in transit:** protect data while it moves (TLS/HTTPS, IPsec, SSH).\n- **Data at rest:** protect stored data (full-disk encryption, encrypted databases and backups).\n- **Symmetric:** one shared key, fast, used for bulk data (**AES**).\n- **Asymmetric:** a public/private key pair (RSA, ECC). TLS uses asymmetric crypto to agree on a session key, then symmetric crypto for the data.\n- **Hashing:** a fixed-length digest that proves **integrity** (SHA-256; MD5 and SHA-1 are broken).\n- **Digital signature:** a hash signed with the sender's private key. It proves **authenticity, integrity, and non-repudiation**.\n- **Perfect forward secrecy:** ephemeral keys per session mean a stolen long-term key can't decrypt past sessions.\n\n## PKI and key management\n\n- A **certificate authority (CA)** signs certificates that bind a public key to an identity (X.509). Root CAs are kept offline; intermediate CAs issue certificates.\n- **Revocation** is checked with a **CRL** or **OCSP**. **Self-signed** certificates aren't trusted by default.\n- **Key management** covers generating, storing (**HSMs**, vaults), distributing, **rotating**, and revoking keys.\n\n## Identity and access management (IAM)\n\nIAM manages identities, authentication, authorization, and the account **life cycle**, including prompt **deprovisioning**.\n\n**AAA:**\n- **Authentication:** who you are.\n- **Authorization:** what you're allowed to do.\n- **Accounting:** what you did, logged.\n\n**MFA:** combine *different* factor types:\n- Something you **know** (password, PIN).\n- Something you **have** (phone, token, smart card).\n- Something you **are** (biometrics).\n- Location-based factors, such as somewhere you are.\n\nTwo passwords is still single-factor.\n\n**Time-based authentication:**\n- **TOTP** codes change about every 30 seconds and need synced clocks. **HOTP** is counter-based.\n- **Time-of-day restrictions** limit when an account can log in.\n\n**SSO (single sign-on):** log in once and reach many applications.\n- **SAML:** XML assertions from an **identity provider (IdP)** to a **service provider (SP)**.\n- **OAuth 2.0** handles delegated authorization; **OpenID Connect** adds identity on top.\n- **Kerberos:** ticket-based, used in Active Directory.\n\n## Directory and AAA protocols\n\n| Protocol | Ports | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **LDAP / LDAPS** | 389 / 636 | Query directory services (Active Directory) |\n| **RADIUS** | UDP 1812/1813 | Network access AAA (Wi-Fi 802.1X, VPN). Encrypts only the password |\n| **TACACS+** | TCP 49 | Device administration. Encrypts the **entire payload** and separates the A's (per-command authorization) |\n\n**802.1X** roles: the **supplicant** (client), the **authenticator** (switch or AP), and the **authentication server** (RADIUS).\n\n## Authorization models\n\n- **Least privilege:** only the access a job requires.\n- **RBAC (role-based):** permissions are assigned to roles, and users are assigned to roles.\n- **MAC (mandatory):** access set by security labels such as Secret or Top Secret.\n- **DAC (discretionary):** the owner decides who has access.\n- **ABAC (attribute-based):** decisions use attributes such as department, device, location, and time.\n- **PAM:** vaulted, monitored, just-in-time privileged accounts.\n\n## Geofencing\n\nAllow or deny access based on location (GPS or IP geolocation). For example, block logins from countries you don't operate in. **Impossible travel** alerts flag logins from distant places too close together in time.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Encrypts the whole payload, device admin\" → TACACS+. \"Wi-Fi/VPN user auth\" → RADIUS. \"Log in once, use many apps\" → SSO (SAML). \"Smart card + PIN\" → MFA.","4|Physical security":"# Physical security\n\nPhysical access can bypass logical controls. An attacker at the console can reset passwords, plug in rogue devices, or steal equipment.\n\n## Cameras\n\n- **Purpose:** **deter** (visible cameras) and **detect and record** evidence for investigations.\n- **Types:**\n  - **PTZ** (pan-tilt-zoom) cameras can be steered to follow activity.\n  - **Infrared** cameras record in low light.\n  - Motion-triggered recording saves storage.\n- **Placement:** cover **entrances, server rooms, aisles, and rack rows** with no blind spots, and protect and retain the recordings.\n\n## Locks and entry control\n\n| Control | How it works |\n|---|---|\n| **Badge / proximity card reader** | Something you have. Logs every entry; lost badges can be disabled instantly |\n| **Biometric lock** | Fingerprint, face, or iris; tied to the person, not a shareable credential |\n| **Cipher lock** | Keypad code. Codes can be shared or observed, so rotate them |\n| **Traditional keyed lock** | Simple, but lost keys mean re-keying |\n| **Multifactor door access** | Badge + PIN, or badge + biometric |\n| **Locking racks and cabinets** | Protect devices and console ports in shared spaces |\n| **Cable locks** | Secure laptops to desks |\n\n## Preventing tailgating and other risks\n\n- **Access control vestibule** (formerly \"mantrap\"): two interlocking doors, only one open at a time.\n- **Visitor logs and escorts** for people in sensitive areas.\n- **Security awareness**, so staff don't hold doors open.\n- **Alarm sensors** (door contacts, motion) for after-hours entry.\n- **Bollards** stop vehicles; fences, lighting, and signs deter.\n- **Tamper-evident seals** show whether equipment was opened.\n- **Privacy screens** and a **clean desk policy** help against shoulder surfing and exposed documents.\n- **Faraday cages** block electromagnetic signals.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Stop someone following an employee through a door\" → access control vestibule. \"Revocable entry with an audit trail\" → badge reader. Cameras both **deter** and **detect**.","4|Deception technologies":"# Deception technologies\n\nDeception technologies plant **fake targets** that no legitimate user should ever touch. Any interaction is a strong, low-false-positive sign of an intruder, and it shows defenders the attacker's tools and methods.\n\n## Types\n\n| Technology | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Honeypot** | A single decoy system (fake server or service) that attracts attackers |\n| **Honeynet** | A **network** of honeypots that simulates a realistic environment for deeper observation |\n| **Honeyfile** | A tempting fake document (e.g. \"passwords.xlsx\") that alerts when opened |\n| **Honeytoken** | Fake data, such as a bogus credential, API key, or database record, that alerts when used |\n| **DNS sinkhole / fake records** | Redirects malicious or decoy names to a controlled address |\n\n## Interaction levels\n\n- **Low-interaction:** emulates a few services. Easy and safe, but gathers less intelligence.\n- **High-interaction:** a real system attackers can fully use. Richer intelligence, but more risk.\n\n## Placement and use\n\n- **Internet-facing** honeypots study external attacks.\n- **Internal** honeypots catch **lateral movement** after a breach.\n- **Isolate them carefully** (segmentation, monitoring) so attackers can't use a honeypot to reach real systems.\n- Deception only **observes and alerts**. Never use it to \"hack back\". Involve legal and policy teams, because monitoring has legal implications.\n- **Tarpits** deliberately slow down malicious connections, such as scanners and spam bots.\n\n## Why use it\n\nSignature-based tools need to recognize an attack. Deception doesn't: touching the decoy *is* the alert. That makes it effective against **unknown (zero-day)** techniques.\n\n> **Exam tip:** One decoy → honeypot. Many decoys forming a fake network → honeynet. Fake credential that triggers an alert → honeytoken.","4|Security terminology":"# Security terminology\n\n## Core terms\n\n| Term | Definition | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Vulnerability** | A **weakness** that could be exploited | Unpatched firmware, default password, open port |\n| **Threat** | Anything that could **cause harm** by exploiting a vulnerability | Attacker, malware, insider, flood, fire |\n| **Exploit** | Code or a technique that **takes advantage** of a vulnerability | A script that abuses a buffer overflow |\n| **Risk** | The **likelihood** a threat exploits a vulnerability, combined with its **impact** | High risk = likely and damaging |\n\nRisk ≈ likelihood × impact. Controls reduce one or both.\n\n## The CIA triad\n\n| Principle | Means | Violated by | Protected by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Confidentiality** | Only authorized people can see data | Eavesdropping, data theft | Encryption, access control |\n| **Integrity** | Data is accurate and unaltered | Tampering in transit | Hashing, digital signatures |\n| **Availability** | Systems are usable when needed | DoS/DDoS, outages | Redundancy, backups, DDoS protection |\n\n**Non-repudiation** means someone can't deny an action (digital signatures, audit logs).\n\n## Other key terms\n\n- **Zero-day:** a vulnerability unknown to the vendor or unpatched. Signatures don't exist yet.\n- **CVE:** a public identifier for a known vulnerability (e.g. CVE-2024-12345).\n- **CVSS:** a severity score from 0 to 10, used to prioritize patching.\n- **Attack surface:** every point where an attacker could get in. Shrink it by disabling unused services, ports, and accounts.\n- **Defense in depth:** layered controls (physical, network, host, application, data, people).\n- **Insider threat:** a current or former employee or contractor misusing access.\n- **Vulnerability scan vs penetration test:** a scan finds possible weaknesses; a pen test actively **exploits** them, with written authorization and rules of engagement.\n\n## Risk responses\n\n| Response | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Mitigate** | Add controls to reduce risk |\n| **Transfer** | Shift the financial impact to someone else (e.g. cyber insurance) |\n| **Avoid** | Stop the risky activity entirely |\n| **Accept** | Formally acknowledge the risk (usually the **residual risk** after controls) |\n\n> **Exam tip:** Weakness = vulnerability. Bad actor or event = threat. The attack tool = exploit. DDoS hits **availability**; tampering hits **integrity**; eavesdropping hits **confidentiality**.","4|Audits & compliance":"# Audits & compliance\n\nRegulations and standards set **minimum** security requirements. **Audits** check, with evidence, that controls are in place. Remember: being compliant doesn't automatically mean being secure.\n\n## PCI DSS\n\nThe **Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard** applies to anyone who **stores, processes, or transmits credit card data**. Key network requirements:\n\n- **Network security controls** (firewalls) protecting the **cardholder data environment (CDE)**.\n- **Segmentation** that isolates the CDE, which **shrinks audit scope**.\n- **Strong encryption** for card data sent over open or public networks.\n- Secure configurations: change vendor defaults and harden systems.\n- **Logging and monitoring.** Keep audit logs for at least **one year**, with three months immediately available.\n- Regular vulnerability scans and penetration tests.\n- Never store sensitive authentication data (such as full track data or the CVV) after authorization.\n\n## GDPR\n\nThe **General Data Protection Regulation** protects the **personal data of people in the EU**. It applies to any organization handling that data, wherever the organization is based.\n\n- Lawful basis and consent for processing.\n- Individual rights: access, correction, and the **right to erasure** (\"right to be forgotten\").\n- **Breach notification** to the supervisory authority generally within **72 hours**.\n- Some organizations must appoint a **data protection officer (DPO)**.\n\n## Data locality (data residency)\n\nLaws may require data to be **stored and processed within a specific country or region**. In the cloud, choose **regions in the required jurisdiction** and restrict replication to them.\n\n## Other regulations\n\n- **HIPAA:** US patient health information.\n- **SOX:** financial reporting.\n- **GLBA:** financial institutions.\n- **FERPA:** student records.\n\n## Audit practices\n\n- **Evidence:** auditors need documented policies, change records, configuration baselines, access reviews, and logs.\n- **Firewall rule reviews:** periodically remove unused or overly permissive rules.\n- **Log retention:** meet each regulation's retention period, with accurate time (NTP).\n- **Data classification:** labels such as public, internal, confidential, and restricted drive protection requirements.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Credit cards → **PCI DSS**. EU personal data → **GDPR**. \"Data must stay in-country\" → **data locality**. Segmenting the CDE reduces PCI scope.","4|Network segmentation":"# Network segmentation\n\nSegmentation splits the network into zones (VLANs and subnets) and controls traffic between them with **firewalls and ACLs**. It **limits lateral movement**, contains breaches and malware, reduces compliance scope, and improves performance.\n\n## Segments the exam expects\n\n| Segment | Why isolate it |\n|---|---|\n| **IoT** | Smart TVs, cameras, sensors, and building systems are often poorly secured and rarely patched. Isolate them and allow only the flows they need |\n| **IIoT** | Industrial IoT: connected sensors and devices in factories and utilities |\n| **OT (operational technology)** | Hardware and software controlling physical processes. **Safety and availability** come first |\n| **ICS (industrial control systems)** | Systems that control industrial processes, including SCADA, DCS, and **PLCs** |\n| **SCADA** | Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition: monitoring and control over wide areas (power grids, water treatment) |\n| **Guest** | Visitors get **internet-only** access on a separate SSID/VLAN with client isolation, and never reach internal systems |\n| **BYOD** | Personal devices aren't fully managed. Use a separate segment plus **NAC and MDM** policies |\n\n## OT/ICS considerations\n\n- They often run **legacy protocols with no authentication or encryption** (e.g. Modbus, DNP3).\n- A compromise can cause **physical damage or injury**.\n- Separate IT and OT with an **industrial DMZ**: both sides talk only to systems in the DMZ, never directly to each other.\n- Use **jump hosts** for controlled, audited engineer access.\n- Some highly sensitive systems are **air-gapped**: physically isolated, though removable media can still bring threats in.\n\n## Techniques\n\n- **VLANs and subnets**, with **default-deny** firewall rules between zones that allow only required flows.\n- **Micro-segmentation:** fine-grained policies down to individual workloads. A key part of **zero trust** (assume breach, limit lateral movement).\n- **Trusted vs untrusted zones,** and a **screened subnet** for public-facing services.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Smart devices and cameras\" → separate IoT segment. \"Visitors\" → guest network with internet-only access. \"Personal phones\" → BYOD segment with NAC/MDM. \"Power plant controls\" → OT/ICS/SCADA, isolated and protected.","4|Types of attacks":"# Types of attacks\n\n## Denial of service\n\n- **DoS:** one source overwhelms a target. **DDoS:** many sources, usually a **botnet**. Both attack **availability**.\n- **SYN flood:** half-open TCP connections exhaust the server (mitigation: SYN cookies).\n- **Amplification/reflection:** small spoofed requests to open DNS, NTP, or memcached servers produce large responses aimed at the victim.\n\n## Layer 2 attacks\n\n| Attack | How it works | Defense |\n|---|---|---|\n| **VLAN hopping: switch spoofing** | Attacker negotiates a trunk via DTP and reaches all VLANs | Hard-code access ports, disable DTP |\n| **VLAN hopping: double tagging** | Two 802.1Q tags; the outer (native VLAN) tag is stripped and the inner tag reaches another VLAN | Unused native VLAN, tag the native VLAN |\n| **MAC flooding** | Fake MACs fill the CAM table, so the switch floods frames like a hub and the attacker can sniff | **Port security** (limit MACs per port) |\n| **ARP poisoning/spoofing** | Forged ARP replies link the attacker's MAC to another IP (often the gateway), putting the attacker on-path | **Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI)** |\n| **STP manipulation** | Superior BPDUs make the attacker's device the root bridge | BPDU guard, root guard |\n\n## Spoofing and rogue services\n\n- **DNS poisoning/spoofing:** false DNS records in a resolver cache (or a modified hosts file) send users to malicious sites. Defense: **DNSSEC**.\n- **Rogue DHCP server:** hands out a malicious gateway or DNS server. Defense: **DHCP snooping**. **DHCP starvation** exhausts the address pool first.\n- **Rogue devices:** unauthorized APs, switches, or routers plugged into the network. Defenses: NAC, port security, wireless IDS/IPS.\n- **Evil twin:** a fake AP using the **same SSID** as the real one to capture credentials. Defenses: 802.1X with server certificate validation, WIPS. **Deauthentication** attacks often push users onto it (defense: 802.11w PMF).\n- **IP and MAC spoofing:** forging source addresses.\n\n## On-path attack\n\nFormerly \"man-in-the-middle\". The attacker secretly sits between two parties, intercepting or altering traffic. ARP poisoning, rogue APs, and DNS spoofing are common ways in. Defense: **encryption with proper certificate validation** (TLS, VPN).\n\n## Social engineering\n\n| Attack | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Phishing** | Fraudulent messages that steal information or deliver malware |\n| Spear phishing / whaling | Targeted at specific people / at executives |\n| Vishing / smishing | By voice call / by SMS |\n| **Dumpster diving** | Searching trash for documents and devices |\n| **Shoulder surfing** | Watching someone type or read sensitive information |\n| **Tailgating / piggybacking** | Following an authorized person through a secure door |\n| Pretexting | A made-up scenario to extract information |\n| Baiting | Leaving infected USB drives for someone to plug in |\n\nOther attacks to know: **brute force** and **password spraying** (defense: MFA, lockout) and **ransomware** (defense: offline backups, patching, segmentation).\n\n> **Exam tip:** Match each attack to its Layer 2 defense: MAC flooding → port security; ARP spoofing → DAI; rogue DHCP → DHCP snooping; double tagging → change the native VLAN.","4|Security features & defense":"# Security features & defense\n\n## Device hardening\n\nReduce each device's attack surface:\n- **Change default credentials.** Use strong passwords, central AAA, and MFA.\n- **Disable unused services and ports.** Shut unused switch ports and put them in a parking VLAN.\n- **Use secure protocols:** SSH instead of Telnet, HTTPS instead of HTTP, **SNMPv3** instead of v1/v2c.\n- **Patch firmware** and verify signed images.\n- **Restrict management access** with ACLs to admin subnets, and add a **login banner** (a legal warning).\n- **Authenticate routing protocols** so rogue neighbors can't inject routes.\n\n## Network access control (NAC)\n\nNAC checks **who and what** connects before granting access:\n- **802.1X** authentication via RADIUS, with **dynamic VLAN or ACL** assignment by role.\n- **Posture assessment:** antivirus, patches, and configuration. Non-compliant devices go to a **quarantine/remediation VLAN**.\n- **MAC authentication bypass (MAB):** for devices that can't do 802.1X, such as printers. Weaker, because MACs can be spoofed.\n- **Agent-based vs agentless:** agentless NAC suits IoT and guest devices.\n\n## Access control lists (ACLs)\n\n- **Ordered rules** processed **top-down; the first match wins**. Every ACL ends with an **implicit deny**.\n- **Order matters:** put specific rules before general ones.\n- **Placement:** standard ACLs (source only) go near the destination; **extended ACLs** go **near the source**.\n- **Wildcard masks:** 0.0.0.0 = one host; 0.0.0.255 = a /24.\n- **Review rules regularly** and remove unused or overly broad ones.\n\n## Filtering\n\n- **URL filtering:** block or allow sites by category, reputation, or URL.\n- **Content filtering:** inspect web, email, and files for malware, prohibited content, or data leaks (**DLP**).\n- **Web application firewall (WAF):** protects web apps from SQL injection and XSS.\n- **Egress filtering:** control *outbound* traffic to stop malware callbacks and data exfiltration.\n\n## Zones and the screened subnet\n\n- **Trusted zones** (internal LAN) and **untrusted zones** (internet), with firewalls enforcing **default deny** between them.\n- A **screened subnet** (formerly DMZ) holds **public-facing servers** (web, mail relays, public DNS) between the internet and the internal network. A single \"three-legged\" firewall can create internet, screened subnet, and internal zones.\n\n## Key management\n\nGenerate, store (HSM or vault), distribute, **rotate**, and revoke keys and certificates. Automate renewals to avoid expiry outages.\n\n## Layer 2 protections\n\n- **DHCP snooping:** only trusted ports may send DHCP offers.\n- **Dynamic ARP Inspection:** validates ARP against the snooping table.\n- **Port security, BPDU guard, root guard, storm control.**\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Check device health before granting access\" → NAC posture assessment. \"Public web server location\" → screened subnet. \"Traffic not matched by any rule\" → implicit deny.","5|Troubleshooting methodology":"# Troubleshooting methodology\n\nCompTIA's 7-step method. **Know the order.** Many questions ask \"what should you do next?\"\n\n## The steps\n\n1. **Identify the problem**\n   - Gather information and question users.\n   - Identify symptoms and **determine whether anything has changed**.\n   - Duplicate the problem if possible.\n   - Approach multiple problems **individually**.\n2. **Establish a theory of probable cause**\n   - **Question the obvious** (power, cables, link lights).\n   - Consider multiple approaches: **top-to-bottom** or **bottom-to-top** through the OSI model, or **divide and conquer**.\n3. **Test the theory to determine the cause**\n   - If confirmed, determine the next steps to resolve the problem.\n   - If not confirmed, **establish a new theory or escalate**.\n4. **Establish a plan of action to resolve the problem and identify potential effects**\n   - Include change management, a maintenance window, and a rollback plan.\n5. **Implement the solution or escalate as necessary**\n   - Escalate when the fix is beyond your knowledge, authority, or scope.\n6. **Verify full system functionality and, if applicable, implement preventive measures**\n   - Check the user's real workflow, not just the original symptom.\n   - Add monitoring, redundancy, or training to prevent recurrence.\n7. **Document findings, actions, outcomes, and lessons learned**\n   - **Always the last step.**\n\n> **Memory tip:** **I**dentify, **E**stablish, **T**est, **P**lan, **I**mplement, **V**erify, **D**ocument.\n\n## Approaches\n\n| Approach | When to use |\n|---|---|\n| **Bottom-up** (Layer 1 → 7) | Total loss of connectivity: check cables and link lights first |\n| **Top-down** (Layer 7 → 1) | One application fails but the network seems fine |\n| **Divide and conquer** | Start in the middle (e.g. ping, Layer 3) and go up or down based on the result |\n| **Follow the path** | Trace traffic hop by hop from source to destination |\n| **Spot the difference** | Compare a working system with a failing one |\n\n## Good habits\n\n- **Change one thing at a time** so you know what fixed it.\n- **Ask \"what changed?\"** Check change logs, patches, and moves.\n- **Determine scope:** one user (local cause) or many users (shared infrastructure)?\n- **Intermittent problems:** gather data over time with logs and monitoring, and note exact times.\n- **Don't skip ahead.** Replacing hardware before identifying the problem wastes time and money.\n\n> **Exam tip:** After a theory is confirmed → **establish a plan of action**. After implementing → **verify full functionality**. The final step is always **document**.","5|Cabling & physical interface issues":"# Cabling & physical interface issues\n\n## Cable problems\n\n| Issue | Symptoms / cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Incorrect cable type** | Cat 5e on a 10G run, multimode where single-mode is needed, wrong jacket rating | Use the right category, fiber type, and rating |\n| **Signal degradation: attenuation** | Signal fades over distance; copper over **100 m** gives errors | Shorten the run, add a switch, or use fiber |\n| **Signal degradation: crosstalk** | Signal bleeds between pairs, often from **untwisting too much** at terminations | Re-terminate properly |\n| **Signal degradation: EMI** | Interference from motors, lights, power cables | Reroute, use STP or fiber |\n| **Improper termination** | Opens, shorts, split pairs, reversed or crossed pairs, loose punchdowns | Re-crimp or re-punch; test with a cable tester or certifier |\n| **TX/RX transposed** | A new fiber link stays down even though the optics are fine | **Swap the two fibers** at one end (polarity) |\n| **Dirty or damaged fiber** | Low RX light, errors | Inspect with a fiber scope, clean, check bend radius |\n| **Kinked or crushed cable** | Errors, slow speed, or no link | Replace the cable |\n\nA **split pair** passes continuity tests but has severe crosstalk. Only a cable **certifier** catches it.\n\n## Interface counters and status\n\n| Counter / status | Usually means |\n|---|---|\n| **CRC / FCS errors** increasing | Physical problem: bad cable, EMI, or duplex mismatch |\n| **Runts** (frames under 64 bytes) | Collisions or a faulty NIC |\n| **Giants** (frames over MTU) | MTU or jumbo frame mismatch |\n| **Late collisions** | **Duplex mismatch** or a cable that's too long |\n| **Output drops / discards** | Congestion (queues full) |\n| **Input queue drops** | Device can't keep up (CPU) or traffic bursts |\n| **Up/up** | Working |\n| **Down/down** | Physical: unplugged, bad cable, far end off |\n| **Up/down** (line protocol down) | Layer 2 problem: encapsulation or keepalive mismatch |\n| **Administratively down** | Shut down deliberately (`shutdown`) |\n| **Err-disabled** | Auto-disabled for a violation (port security, BPDU guard, link flapping). Fix the cause, then `shut`/`no shut` |\n\n## Hardware issues\n\n- **PoE:**\n  - The device needs more power than the port's class provides (e.g. a PoE+ AP on an 802.3af port), or the switch's **total PoE budget** is exhausted.\n  - Symptoms: devices won't power on or keep rebooting.\n  - Check with `show power inline`.\n- **Transceiver mismatch:**\n  - SR on one end and LR on the other, wrong speed (an SFP in an SFP+ link), or an unsupported third-party module.\n  - Optics must match on both ends.\n- **Signal strength:**\n  - **Fiber:** check **DOM** RX power against receiver sensitivity. Too weak means dirt, breaks, or wrong fiber; too strong saturates the receiver (add an **attenuator** on short long-reach links).\n  - **Wireless:** low **RSSI** (e.g. weaker than −70 dBm) or low SNR causes slow, dropping connections.\n- **Speed/duplex fallback:** a gigabit link that negotiates only 100 Mbps often has one bad pair.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"CRC errors rising\" → Layer 1 (cable/EMI). \"Late collisions\" → duplex mismatch. \"New fiber link down, optics good\" → TX/RX swapped. \"Port err-disabled after a hub was plugged in\" → port security or BPDU guard.","5|Network services issues":"# Network services issues\n\n## Switching issues\n\n- **STP:**\n  - **Loop or broadcast storm:** without STP, redundant links cause 100% utilization, MAC flapping, and a pegged CPU.\n  - **Wrong root bridge:** a new switch with a lower priority takes over and paths become inefficient. Prevent it with root guard and deliberate priorities.\n  - **Slow port startup:** classic STP takes about 30–50 seconds, so DHCP can time out at boot. Use **PortFast** and BPDU guard on edge ports, or RSTP.\n  - **Blocking ports** pass no data.\n- **VLAN assignment:**\n  - A port in the **wrong VLAN** puts the user in the wrong subnet: wrong IP range, can't reach resources. After fixing it, renew the DHCP lease.\n  - **VLAN not allowed on a trunk** or missing on a switch: hosts can't reach beyond the local switch.\n  - **Native VLAN mismatch:** traffic leaks between VLANs and logs show mismatch errors.\n- **ACLs:**\n  - A new ACL with only deny lines blocks everything, because of the **implicit deny**.\n  - **Rule order:** the first match wins, so a broad deny above a permit blocks the permitted traffic.\n  - **Wrong wildcard mask:** a rule meant for one host blocks a whole subnet.\n\n## Routing issues\n\n- **Missing route:** the destination isn't in the routing table and there's no default route, so packets are **dropped** (\"destination unreachable\").\n- **Wrong or missing default route:** internal traffic works but internet or remote traffic fails.\n- **Routing loop:** traceroute shows the **same hops repeating** until the TTL expires (e.g. two static routes pointing at each other).\n- **Missing return route:** A can reach B, but B has no path back.\n- **Asymmetric routing:** return traffic bypasses the stateful firewall that saw the outbound traffic, so it gets dropped.\n- **Neighbor flapping** (OSPF and others): mismatched timers, area, MTU, or authentication, or an unstable link.\n\n## Address pool exhaustion\n\n- **Symptom:** new devices get **APIPA (169.254.x.x)** while existing devices keep working.\n- **Fix:** **shorten lease times** (especially on guest networks), **expand the scope**, and clear stale leases.\n- **Related problems:**\n  - A **rogue DHCP server** (e.g. a home router someone plugged in) hands out the wrong gateway. Fix with DHCP snooping.\n  - A **missing DHCP relay** means a whole VLAN gets no addresses while other VLANs work.\n\n## Incorrect host settings\n\n| Misconfiguration | Symptom |\n|---|---|\n| **Wrong default gateway** (or one not on the host's subnet) | Local hosts reachable; **nothing off-subnet** works |\n| **Wrong subnet mask** | Some remote hosts are wrongly treated as local (or the reverse); partial reachability |\n| **Wrong IP** (wrong network) | Can't talk to anything |\n| **Duplicate IP** | \"IP address conflict\" warnings and intermittent connectivity. Often a static IP inside the DHCP range; use exclusions or reservations |\n| **Wrong DNS server** | Works by IP, **fails by name** |\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Reach local, not remote\" → default gateway. \"Works by IP, not by name\" → DNS. \"New devices get 169.254.x.x\" → DHCP scope exhausted (or DHCP unreachable). \"Same hops repeat in traceroute\" → routing loop.","5|Performance issues":"# Performance issues\n\n## Key metrics\n\n| Metric | Meaning | Hurts |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Bandwidth** | Theoretical link capacity | — |\n| **Throughput** | Data rate actually achieved (always lower) | Big transfers |\n| **Latency** | Delay from source to destination | Interactive apps, VoIP |\n| **Jitter** | **Variation** in delay | VoIP and video (choppy, robotic audio) |\n| **Packet loss** | Packets that never arrive | Real-time media, TCP throughput |\n\n**VoIP targets:** one-way latency of **≤150 ms**, jitter under about 30 ms, loss under about 1%.\n\n## Congestion\n\n- **Cause:** demand exceeds capacity, typically at a **bottleneck** such as a slower WAN link or where a 10G link feeds a 1G link.\n- **Symptoms:** utilization near 100%, rising latency, **output drops**, slowness at peak times.\n- **Fixes:**\n  - **QoS:** classify and mark traffic (DSCP, 802.1p), then give voice a **priority queue** (LLQ).\n  - **Shaping** (buffer to a rate) or **policing** (drop above a rate).\n  - Schedule backups and updates off-hours.\n  - Upgrade the bottleneck link.\n- QoS only matters **when there's congestion**. On an idle link it changes little.\n\n## Latency\n\n- **Components:** propagation (distance) + serialization + processing + queuing.\n- **More bandwidth doesn't reduce propagation delay.** Geostationary satellite adds about 600 ms round trip.\n- **Other causes:** overloaded devices (high CPU), deep queues (**bufferbloat**), and VPN hairpinning to distant concentrators.\n- **TCP over long distances:** throughput is limited by window size ÷ round-trip time. Use window scaling or WAN optimization.\n\n## Packet loss\n\n- **Causes:** congestion, bad cables or optics (check CRC errors), overloaded devices, wireless interference.\n- **Tools:**\n  - **pathping** (Windows) and **mtr** show per-hop loss.\n  - **iperf** tests throughput, jitter, and loss between two hosts.\n\n## Wireless interference\n\n| Problem | Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Co-channel interference** | Nearby APs on the **same** channel share airtime | Channel plan, lower power, narrower channels |\n| **Adjacent-channel interference** | **Overlapping** channels (e.g. 3 and 5 in 2.4 GHz) | Use 1, 6, 11 |\n| **Non-Wi-Fi interference** | Microwaves, Bluetooth, cordless phones (2.4 GHz); radar on DFS channels (5 GHz) | Use a **spectrum analyzer**; move to 5/6 GHz |\n| **Absorption / reflection / multipath** | Walls, water, metal shelving | Reposition APs, survey |\n| **Weak signal / low SNR** | Too far from the AP | Add or move APs. Aim for SNR ≥ about 20–25 dB |\n| **Overcrowding** | Too many clients per AP | More APs, 5/6 GHz, airtime fairness |\n| **Sticky clients** | Clients hold on to a distant AP | 802.11k/v/r, right-size AP power |\n| **Legacy clients** | Slow data rates eat airtime | Disable low data rates, separate SSID |\n\nCalculating SNR: signal −65 dBm with noise −90 dBm → **25 dB**.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Robotic VoIP but downloads fine\" → jitter, fix with QoS. \"Slow every day at 9 a.m.\" → congestion at peak. \"Microwave kills Wi-Fi at lunch\" → 2.4 GHz interference.","5|Tools & protocols":"# Tools & protocols\n\n## Protocol analyzers\n\n**Wireshark** and **tcpdump** capture and decode packets. You can see handshakes, retransmissions, errors, and exactly where a conversation fails.\n- Get traffic from a **SPAN** port or a **TAP**.\n- **Display filters** (Wireshark): `dns`, `http`, `tcp.port == 443`, `ip.addr == 10.1.1.5`.\n- **Capture filters** (tcpdump, BPF syntax): `host 10.0.0.5 and port 53`. Write to a file with `-w capture.pcap`.\n- **What to look for:**\n  - **SYN with no reply:** host unreachable or a firewall is dropping traffic.\n  - **Immediate RST:** nothing is listening on that port.\n  - **Many retransmissions:** packet loss.\n\n## Command-line tools\n\n| Tool | Use |\n|---|---|\n| **ping** | Reachability and round-trip time over ICMP. Windows options: `-t` continuous, `-n` count, `-l` size, `-f` don't fragment (MTU testing) |\n| **tracert** (Windows) / **traceroute** (Linux) | Each hop to a destination, using increasing TTLs. Linux sends UDP probes by default |\n| **pathping** / **mtr** | Traceroute plus per-hop loss statistics |\n| **ipconfig /all** / **ip addr** | IP, mask, gateway, DNS, MAC, DHCP lease |\n| `ipconfig /release` + `/renew` | Get a new DHCP lease |\n| `ipconfig /flushdns`, `/displaydns` | Clear or view the DNS cache |\n| **nslookup** / **dig** | Query DNS. Query a specific server with e.g. `nslookup host 8.8.8.8` |\n| **arp -a** / **ip neigh** | IP-to-MAC table, for duplicate IPs and spoofing |\n| **netstat -ano** / **ss -tulnp** | Active connections, listening ports, owning process |\n| **route print** / **ip route** | Routing table and default gateway |\n| **nmap** | Discover hosts and open ports (only with authorization) |\n| **tcpdump** | Command-line packet capture |\n| **iperf** | Throughput, jitter, and loss between two endpoints |\n| **Test-NetConnection -Port** / **nc -zv** | Test whether a specific TCP port is reachable |\n\n## Device commands (Cisco-style)\n\n- `show ip interface brief`: interface IPs and up/down status.\n- `show interfaces`: counters (CRC, collisions, drops). `show interfaces status`: speed, duplex, VLAN, err-disabled.\n- `show vlan brief`, `show interfaces trunk`: VLAN membership and trunk allowed or native VLANs.\n- `show mac address-table`: which port a MAC was learned on.\n- `show ip route`, `show arp`: routing and ARP tables.\n- `show spanning-tree`: root bridge, port roles and states.\n- `show cdp neighbors` / `show lldp neighbors`: directly connected devices.\n- `show logging`: device logs.\n\n## Hardware tools\n\n| Tool | Use |\n|---|---|\n| **Cable tester** (wire map) | Opens, shorts, reversed and crossed pairs |\n| **Cable certifier** | Proves a run meets its category spec (NEXT, loss, length) |\n| **TDR / OTDR** | Distance to a fault in copper / fiber |\n| **Toner and probe** | Find a specific cable in a bundle or at a patch panel |\n| **Optical power meter** | Measure fiber light levels and loss |\n| **Fiber inspection scope** | Check end faces before connecting |\n| **Loopback plug** | Test that a port or NIC can send and receive |\n| **Multimeter / PoE tester** | Voltage and PoE power |\n| **Punchdown tool / crimper** | Re-terminate jacks, panels, RJ45 plugs |\n\n## Wi-Fi analyzers\n\n- **Wi-Fi analyzer:** decodes 802.11. Shows SSIDs, BSSIDs, channels, RSSI and SNR, and security settings. Use it for site surveys and channel planning.\n- **Spectrum analyzer:** shows **raw RF energy**, including **non-Wi-Fi interference** (microwaves, Bluetooth, radar) that a Wi-Fi analyzer can't see.\n\n> **Exam tip:** \"Ping works by IP, not name\" → nslookup/dig. \"Find which cable in the bundle\" → toner. \"Locate a fiber break\" → OTDR. \"Microwave interference\" → spectrum analyzer."};
